{{/*
  ==========================================================================================================================
  gitea-board-badges — footer.tmpl
  ==========================================================================================================================

  WHAT THIS DOES
  --------------
  Gitea (our self-hosted Git server) has project boards: kanban boards with columns such as "To Do", "In Progress" and
  "Done", on which issues and pull requests are placed as cards. Gitea's own issue list, however, does not show in which
  column an issue currently is. This file adds that, in two ways:

    1. Badges: next to the title of every issue / pull request in a list, a coloured label with the name of its board
       column (in the colour of that column on the board). Clicking the label opens the board.
    2. Filter: a "Status" dropdown in Gitea's filter bar (next to Label, Milestone, Project, Author ...). Choosing a
       status shows every item of the whole list that is in that column, across all pages of the list. "No board"
       shows the items that are on no board.

  It works on every page that has an issue list: the issue and pull request lists of a repository, and the global
  "/issues" overview, which can mix issues from several repositories.

  HOW IT GETS INTO GITEA
  ----------------------
  Gitea has no plugin system, but it lets administrators add their own templates. Gitea includes the template
  "custom/footer.tmpl" at the bottom of every page it renders. This file is placed (or mounted read-only, with Docker) at
  $GITEA_CUSTOM/templates/custom/footer.tmpl; in the official Docker image that is /data/gitea/templates/custom/footer.tmpl.
  Gitea reads templates only when it starts, so after a change Gitea has to be restarted. See README.md.

  This block is a Go template comment: Gitea removes it before sending the page, so this explanation costs the visitor
  nothing. Everything below it (the <script>) is sent to the browser and runs there.

  HOW IT WORKS (SHORT VERSION)
  ----------------------------
    a. Find the issue list on the page. No list: do nothing.
    b. For the items on the current page: download the project boards of their repository, read which issue is in
       which column, and add the badges. This happens right away, so the badges appear quickly.
    c. At the same time, if the list has more than one page: download the other pages of the same list (with the same
       Gitea filters and sorting), add their items to the list, hidden, and give them badges too.
    d. When everything is loaded, build the "Status" dropdown and apply the filter from the URL, if any.
         - no status chosen: the list looks exactly like Gitea's own page, with Gitea's page buttons;
         - a status chosen: all items of the whole list with that status are shown, in Gitea's order, and Gitea's page
           buttons are hidden, because they would no longer match what is shown.

  DESIGN CHOICES
  --------------
  * Read-only and private. The script only reads pages the visitor can already see, with the visitor's own login and
    permissions. It never changes anything in Gitea and sends nothing outside our Gitea server.
  * Board pages instead of the API. Gitea's REST API does have endpoints for boards, but it does not accept the normal
    browser login (it answers 404 for private repositories), only API tokens. The board web pages do work with the
    browser login, so the script downloads those and reads the HTML.
  * Whole list, by reading Gitea's own list pages. To filter the whole list, the script downloads the other pages of the
    list that is open (same address, other "page=" number). That way Gitea's own filters (open/closed, labels, search
    text, sorting ...) keep applying, and the items look exactly like Gitea's. Each page is one extra request, made one
    after the other in the background; for safety at most MAX_PAGES pages are read.
  * Filter in the URL. The chosen status is stored after the "#" in the address (e.g. ".../issues#status=To%20Do").
    That part of the URL never goes to the server, so Gitea's own filters and paging are not affected, and a filtered
    list can be bookmarked or shared.
  * Fails quietly. If Gitea changes its HTML in an update and the script cannot find what it needs, it simply adds
    nothing; Gitea itself keeps working. Run test/test.sh before a Gitea update to find out in advance.

  GITEA HTML THE SCRIPT DEPENDS ON (check these after a Gitea update)
  ------------------------------------------------------------------
    Issue list:   #issue-list > .item          one item (issue or pull request) in the list
                  a.list-item-large-title      the title link of an item, e.g. /owner/repo/issues/12
                  .labels-list                 the place where Gitea shows an item's labels; the badge goes there
                  .page.buttons                Gitea's page buttons under the list (only there when it has more pages)
                  ?page=N                      the address parameter Gitea uses for page N of a list
    Filter bar:   .project-filter              Gitea's own "Project" dropdown; ours goes right after it
                  .list-header-filters         the filter bar on /issues (which has no Project dropdown)
    Board page:   .project-column              one column; its colour is in style="background: #rrggbb"
                  .project-column-title-text   the column name
                  .issue-card                  one card (issue or pull request) in a column

  TEXTS
  -----
  The few visible texts are in the TEXTS table at the top of the script, in English and Dutch. The script picks the
  language the visitor has chosen in Gitea (Gitea puts it in <html lang="...">) and falls back to English. To add a
  language, add an entry to TEXTS.
  ==========================================================================================================================
*/}}
{{/* The nonce is a random value Gitea generates for every page. Gitea's Content Security Policy only allows inline
     scripts that carry the right nonce, so without it the browser could refuse to run this script. */}}
<script nonce="{{ctx.CspScriptNonce}}">
// Everything is wrapped in a function that runs immediately, so none of our variables leak into the page's global scope
// and cannot clash with Gitea's own JavaScript.
(() => {
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // a. Is there an issue list on this page?
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // This template is included on every Gitea page (code, settings, wiki ...). Only pages with an issue list are relevant;
  // on all other pages we stop right here, so the script costs practically nothing there.
  const list = document.querySelector('#issue-list');
  if (!list) return;

  // If Gitea runs under a sub-path (e.g. https://example.com/git/), every link starts with that path. Gitea exposes it
  // as window.config.appSubUrl. When Gitea runs at the root of its domain, this is an empty string.
  const base = (window.config && window.config.appSubUrl) || '';

  // Safety limit for reading the other pages of a long list: at most this many pages (with Gitea's default of 20 items
  // per page that is 1000 items). Beyond that the filter covers the first MAX_PAGES pages and says so.
  const MAX_PAGES = 50;

  // The visible texts, per language. Gitea writes the visitor's chosen language into <html lang="..."> (e.g. "en-US",
  // "nl-NL"); we use the part before the "-" and fall back to English for languages that are not in this table.
  //   status: the dropdown button without a filter        all:  the entry that removes the filter
  //   none:   the entry for items that are on no board     header / headerFirst: heading at the top of the open dropdown
  const TEXTS = {
    en: {status: 'Status', all: 'All', none: 'No board', header: 'Status on the board (all pages)',
      headerFirst: (n) => `Status on the board (first ${n} pages)`},
    nl: {status: 'Status', all: 'Alle', none: 'Geen bord', header: "Status op het bord (alle pagina's)",
      headerFirst: (n) => `Status op het bord (eerste ${n} pagina's)`},
  };
  const T = TEXTS[(document.documentElement.lang || 'en').split('-')[0].toLowerCase()] || TEXTS.en;

  // The filter value used for items that are on no board at all. It is also what the user sees for that entry.
  const NONE = T.none;

  // Helper: download a Gitea web page and turn its HTML into a document we can search with querySelector, exactly like
  // the page in the browser. "same-origin" sends the visitor's login cookie along, so we see what the visitor may see.
  // Returns null when the page cannot be read (e.g. no access), so callers can simply skip it.
  const page = async (url) => {
    const r = await fetch(url, {credentials: 'same-origin'});
    return r.ok ? new DOMParser().parseFromString(await r.text(), 'text/html') : null;
  };

  // Helper: choose a readable text colour for a given background colour. It computes the perceived brightness of the
  // colour (the standard weights for red, green and blue) and returns dark text on light colours such as the yellow of
  // "Done", and white text on dark colours such as the blue of "To Do".
  const textColor = (hex) => {
    const n = parseInt(hex.replace('#', ''), 16);   // "#fbca04" -> one number holding red, green and blue
    const brightness = 0.299 * (n >> 16) + 0.587 * ((n >> 8) & 255) + 0.114 * (n & 255);   // 0 (black) .. 255 (white)
    return brightness > 150 ? '#1f1f1f' : '#ffffff';
  };

  // Each item's title links to the issue or pull request, e.g. "/alice/website/issues/12". From that link we take
  // the owner ("alice"), the repository ("website") and the number (12). Pull requests and issues share one
  // numbering per repository, so "owner/repo" plus the number identifies an item.
  const re = new RegExp(`^${base}/([^/]+)/([^/]+)/(?:issues|pulls)/(\\d+)$`);
  // Returns {repo: "owner/repo", number: 12, id: "/owner/repo/issues/12"} for a row of the list, or null if the row has
  // no title link we understand (such rows are left alone).
  const parse = (item) => {
    const a = item.querySelector('a.list-item-large-title');
    const path = a && new URL(a.getAttribute('href'), location.href).pathname;
    const m = path && path.match(re);
    return m ? {repo: `${m[1]}/${m[2]}`, number: Number(m[3]), id: path} : null;
  };

  // columns: every column name found on the boards, with its colour, in the order the columns appear on the board
  // (To Do, In Progress, Done ...). The dropdown is built from this. If two boards have a column with the same name, they
  // share one entry in the dropdown (the first colour wins).
  const columns = new Map();

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Reading the boards of a repository.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // boards: "owner/repo" -> a promise of what its boards say. Kept so every repository's boards are downloaded only once,
  // even when its items appear on several pages of the list.
  const boards = new Map();
  const boardsOf = (key) => {
    if (!boards.has(key)) boards.set(key, readBoards(key));
    return boards.get(key);
  };

  // Download all open boards of one repository. Returns:
  //   where:  issue number -> list of {project, column, color, href} (a list, because an issue can be on several boards)
  //   multi:  true when the repository has more than one board (badges then also show the board's name)
  const readBoards = async (key) => {
    const where = new Map();
    // The page /owner/repo/projects lists the repository's open boards. We only need the links to the boards in it,
    // which look like /owner/repo/projects/7. A Set removes duplicates (Gitea may link to a board more than once).
    const overview = await page(`${base}/${key}/projects`);
    if (!overview) return {where, multi: false};   // projects page not readable (e.g. projects disabled): no badges
    const boardLink = new RegExp(`^${base}/${key}/projects/\\d+$`);
    const links = new Set([...overview.querySelectorAll('a[href]')].map((a) => a.getAttribute('href'))
      .filter((h) => boardLink.test(h)));

    for (const href of links) {
      // Download one board. Boards are read one after the other (not all at once) to keep the load on Gitea low.
      const board = await page(href);
      if (!board) continue;
      // The board's name is the first part of the page title, e.g. "Roadmap - Projects - ..." -> "Roadmap".
      const project = (board.querySelector('title')?.textContent || '').split(' - ')[0].trim();

      for (const col of board.querySelectorAll('.project-column')) {
        // Column name, e.g. "To Do".
        const column = col.querySelector('.project-column-title-text')?.textContent.trim() || '';
        // Column colour: Gitea puts it in the column's style attribute as "background: #0052cc". A column without a
        // colour gets a neutral grey.
        const color = ((col.getAttribute('style') || '').match(/background:\s*(#[0-9a-fA-F]{6})/) || [])[1] || '#767676';
        // Remember the column for the dropdown (only the first time we see this name, which keeps the board order).
        if (column && !columns.has(column)) columns.set(column, {color});

        // Every card in this column links to its issue or pull request; take the number from that link.
        for (const card of col.querySelectorAll('.issue-card')) {
          const link = card.querySelector('a[href*="/issues/"], a[href*="/pulls/"]')?.getAttribute('href') || '';
          const m = link.match(/\/(?:issues|pulls)\/(\d+)$/);
          if (!m) continue;
          const n = Number(m[1]);
          if (!where.has(n)) where.set(n, []);
          where.get(n).push({project, column, color, href});
        }
      }
    }
    return {where, multi: links.size > 1};
  };

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Adding the badge(s) to one row of the list.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The row also remembers its column(s) in item.boardColumns, which the filter uses later. Rows on no board get NONE.
  const decorate = async (item) => {
    const info = parse(item);
    if (!info) return;
    const {where, multi} = await boardsOf(info.repo);
    const found = where.get(info.number) || [];
    item.boardColumns = found.length ? [...new Set(found.map((w) => w.column))] : [NONE];

    // Put the badge where Gitea shows the item's labels; if Gitea ever drops that element, fall back to the title row.
    const target = item.querySelector('.labels-list') || item.querySelector('.item-header > div');
    for (const w of found) {
      const badge = document.createElement('a');
      // "ui label" is Gitea's own label style, so the badge looks like a normal Gitea label.
      // "board-column-badge" is our own class name, used by the test to find the badges.
      badge.className = 'ui label board-column-badge';
      badge.href = w.href;                          // clicking the badge opens the board
      badge.title = `${w.project}: ${w.column}`;    // tooltip on hover, always with the board name
      // With more than one board in the repository, the badge also says which board, e.g. "Roadmap: To Do".
      badge.textContent = multi ? `${w.project}: ${w.column}` : w.column;
      badge.style.backgroundColor = w.color;
      badge.style.color = textColor(w.color);
      badge.style.marginLeft = '4px';
      target.prepend(badge);   // first in the label area, before the item's normal labels
    }
  };

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // b. The rows of the current page: badges right away.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // "original" = the rows Gitea put on this page. Without a status filter exactly these are shown, as Gitea intended.
  const original = [...list.querySelectorAll(':scope > .item')];
  const current = original.map((item) => decorate(item).catch((e) => console.warn('board column badge:', e)));

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // c. The other pages of the same list, in the background.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Gitea shows its page buttons (".page.buttons") only when the list has more than one page. No buttons: nothing to do.
  const pager = document.querySelector('.page.buttons');
  let truncated = false;   // becomes true when the list has more than MAX_PAGES pages

  const loadOtherPages = async () => {
    if (!pager) return [];
    // The page number of the current page, from the address (?page=3); no number means page 1.
    const here = Number(new URL(location.href).searchParams.get('page')) || 1;
    // Items already in the list, by their link, so an item never appears twice (e.g. when an issue is updated while we
    // read the pages and moves from one page to the next).
    const seen = new Set(original.map((item) => parse(item)?.id).filter(Boolean));
    const before = [], after = [];   // rows of the pages before and after the current page, in Gitea's order

    // The number of the last page. Gitea's page buttons link to pages with "?page=N"; the highest N among them is the
    // last page (Gitea has a "Last" button that points to it). Without such links (should not happen) we fall back to
    // MAX_PAGES and stop at the first page that brings nothing new.
    const numbers = [...pager.querySelectorAll('a[href]')]
      .map((a) => Number(new URL(a.getAttribute('href'), location.href).searchParams.get('page')) || 0);
    const last = numbers.length ? Math.max(here, ...numbers) : MAX_PAGES;
    if (last > MAX_PAGES) truncated = true;   // more pages than we are willing to read

    for (let n = 1; n <= Math.min(last, MAX_PAGES); n++) {
      if (n === here) continue;   // this page is already on screen
      // The same address as the current page (so the same Gitea filters and sorting), only with another page number.
      // The "#..." part (our own filter) is left out: the server does not need it.
      const url = new URL(location.href);
      url.hash = '';
      url.searchParams.set('page', String(n));
      const doc = await page(url.pathname + url.search);
      const rows = doc ? [...doc.querySelectorAll('#issue-list > .item')] : [];
      let added = 0;
      for (const row of rows) {
        const id = parse(row)?.id;
        if (!id || seen.has(id)) continue;
        seen.add(id);
        added++;
        // Copy the row from the downloaded page into this page, hidden, and mark it as coming from another page.
        const copy = document.importNode(row, true);
        copy.dataset.boardOtherPage = String(n);
        copy.style.display = 'none';
        (n < here ? before : after).push(copy);
      }
      // A page after the current one that brings nothing new means we have passed the end of the list. (Gitea answers a
      // page number beyond the last page with the last page again, so "nothing new" is the reliable end signal.)
      if (n > here && !added) break;
    }
    // Put the rows in Gitea's order: earlier pages before the current page's rows, later pages after them.
    const first = original[0] || null;
    for (const row of before) list.insertBefore(row, first);
    for (const row of after) list.append(row);
    return [...before, ...after];
  };

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // d. The "Status" dropdown, built once everything is loaded.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // all = every row: the ones of this page plus the ones of the other pages.
  const filterDropdown = (all) => {
    // None of the repositories in this list has a board: a status filter would be meaningless, so show nothing.
    if (!columns.size) return;

    // Rows whose boards could not be read have no columns yet; treat them as "no board".
    for (const item of all) item.boardColumns ||= [NONE];

    // How many rows of the whole list are in column c (shown next to each choice in the dropdown).
    const count = (c) => all.filter((item) => item.boardColumns.includes(c)).length;

    // Small helper to create an HTML element with an optional class and text.
    const el = (tag, cls, text) => {
      const e = document.createElement(tag);
      if (cls) e.className = cls;
      if (text) e.textContent = text;
      return e;
    };

    // A small coloured dot shown in front of each column in the dropdown. Without a colour (used for "no board") it is
    // an empty circle with a thin border.
    const dot = (color) => {
      const d = el('span', 'board-column-dot');
      Object.assign(d.style, {display: 'inline-block', width: '10px', height: '10px', borderRadius: '50%', flexShrink: '0',
        backgroundColor: color || 'transparent', border: color ? 'none' : '1px solid var(--color-secondary-dark-4)'});
      return d;
    };

    // The dropdown uses exactly the same HTML structure and CSS classes as Gitea's own filter dropdowns (Label,
    // Milestone, Project ...), so it looks identical. Gitea's JavaScript notices new elements with the classes
    // "ui dropdown" by itself and makes them open and close, so we do not need any code for that.
    //   <div class="item ui dropdown jump">   the button in the filter bar
    //     <span class="text">Status</span>    the button text
    //     <svg ...>                            the small triangle
    //     <div class="menu">...</div>          the list that opens
    const dropdown = el('div', 'item ui dropdown jump board-column-filter');
    const label = el('span', 'text');
    dropdown.append(label);
    // The triangle icon, copied from Gitea's own "octicon-triangle-down" icon.
    dropdown.insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend', '<svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" class="svg octicon-triangle-down dropdown icon" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m4.427 7.427 3.396 3.396a.25.25 0 0 0 .354 0l3.396-3.396A.25.25 0 0 0 11.396 7H4.604a.25.25 0 0 0-.177.427"></path></svg>');
    const menu = el('div', 'menu flex-items-menu');
    // Heading at the top of the open list. It says the filter covers all pages, or only the first MAX_PAGES pages of an
    // extremely long list.
    menu.append(el('div', 'header', truncated ? T.headerFirst(MAX_PAGES) : T.header));
    dropdown.append(menu);

    // entries: filter value -> the menu entry for it. The value '' means "all" (no filter).
    const entries = new Map();

    // Add one entry to the menu.
    //   value = the filter value ('' for all, a column name, or NONE)
    //   text  = what the user sees
    //   color = colour of the dot in front of it
    //   n     = number of rows, shown on the right
    const entry = (value, text, color, n) => {
      const a = el('a', 'item board-column-filter-item');
      // A real link to the filtered address, so "open in new tab" and copying the link also work.
      a.href = value ? `#status=${encodeURIComponent(value)}` : '#';
      a.dataset.column = value;   // used by the test to find the entry
      if (value) a.append(dot(value === NONE ? null : color));
      a.append(el('span', 'gt-ellipsis', text));
      if (n !== undefined) a.append(el('span', 'tw-ml-auto tw-text-text-light', String(n)));
      // On a normal click we apply the filter ourselves instead of following the link; preventDefault stops the browser
      // from jumping to the top of the page, which following a "#" link would do. Gitea closes the menu by itself.
      a.addEventListener('click', (e) => { e.preventDefault(); apply(value); });
      entries.set(value, a);
      menu.append(a);
    };

    // The menu: "All", a divider line, every column in board order, and finally "No board".
    entry('', T.all, null, all.length);
    menu.append(el('div', 'divider'));
    for (const [c, {color}] of columns) entry(c, c, color, count(c));
    entry(NONE, NONE, null, count(NONE));

    // Where to put the dropdown:
    //   - repository lists have Gitea's "Project" dropdown: put ours right after it;
    //   - the global /issues page has a filter bar without "Project": put ours first in that bar;
    //   - if neither exists (e.g. Gitea changed its layout), put it in a row of its own just above the list.
    const project = document.querySelector('.project-filter');
    const bar = document.querySelector('.list-header-filters');
    if (project) project.after(dropdown);
    else if (bar) bar.prepend(dropdown);
    else {
      const row = el('div', 'ui secondary menu tw-m-0 tw-mb-2');
      row.append(dropdown);
      list.before(row);
    }

    // Read the current filter from the address, e.g. "#status=To%20Do" -> "To Do". Returns '' when there is none.
    // "#kolom=" is the older name of the same setting (the dropdown used to be called "Kolom"); links that people saved
    // with it keep working.
    const fromAddress = () => decodeURIComponent((location.hash.match(/^#(?:status|kolom)=(.*)$/) || [])[1] || '');

    // Apply a filter value: show/hide rows and Gitea's page buttons, mark the chosen entry, update the button text and
    // the address.
    const apply = (value) => {
      // A column name in the address that does not exist in this list (e.g. an old link): show everything.
      if (value && !entries.has(value)) value = '';

      // Write the choice into the address without reloading the page and without adding a "back" step to the browser
      // history. Without a filter the "#..." part is removed completely.
      history.replaceState(null, '', value ? `#status=${encodeURIComponent(value)}` : location.pathname + location.search);

      // Which rows to show (display '' = normal, 'none' = hidden):
      //   - no filter: only the rows Gitea put on this page, exactly as Gitea shows them;
      //   - a filter:  every row of the whole list, from any page, that is in the chosen column.
      for (const item of all) {
        const show = value ? item.boardColumns.includes(value) : !item.dataset.boardOtherPage;
        item.style.display = show ? '' : 'none';
      }
      // Gitea's page buttons only make sense for Gitea's own pages; with a filter they would be misleading.
      if (pager) pager.style.display = value ? 'none' : '';

      // Mark the chosen entry in the menu, the same way Gitea marks the chosen entry in its own filters.
      for (const [v, a] of entries) a.classList.toggle('selected', v === value);

      // Button text: "Status" without a filter; with a filter the coloured dot plus the column name, in bold.
      label.replaceChildren();
      if (value) {
        const d = dot(value === NONE ? null : columns.get(value)?.color);
        d.style.marginRight = '4px';
        label.append(d);
      }
      label.append(value ? value : T.status);
      label.style.fontWeight = value ? '600' : '';
    };

    // If the user changes the "#..." part by hand, or uses the back button to it, apply that filter too.
    window.addEventListener('hashchange', () => apply(fromAddress()));

    // Apply the filter that was in the address when the page was opened (e.g. from a bookmark).
    apply(fromAddress());
  };

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Start.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The badges of the current page (b, already started above) and the other pages (c) load at the same time. When both
  // are finished, and the other pages have their badges too, the dropdown is built (d).
  // An error is written to the browser console and never shown to the user: at worst some badges are missing, or the
  // filter only covers the current page.
  const others = loadOtherPages()
    .then((rows) => Promise.all(rows.map((row) => decorate(row).catch((e) => console.warn('board column badge:', e))))
      .then(() => rows))
    .catch((e) => { console.warn('board column pages:', e); return []; });
  Promise.all([others, ...current])
    .then(([rows]) => filterDropdown([...original, ...rows]))
    .catch((e) => console.warn('board column filter:', e));
})();
</script>
